es `cursor`, the offset to pass as the next `after`. Without `after`, `wait` returns the next such event appended after the call. With no `timeout_ms` it blocks until an event arrives, and if the endpoint vanishes first the caller gets `instance-unreachable`. On timeout it returns `ok` with `{"timed_out":true}`. `last` returns the `last_assistant_message` of the newest `turn-ended` event. The wrapper holds it in memory and rebuilds it from `events.ndjson` on start. Endpoint names are a short hash of the instance, never the raw `VIOLA_NAME` + path. On Windows the client connects only through the SQOS Identification open (windows-sys `CreateFileW` with `SECURITY_SQOS_PRESENT | SECURITY_IDENTIFICATION | FILE_FLAG_OVERLAPPED`, adopted by `Stream::try_from`), never interprocess's default connect, so a squatted or rogue server cannot impersonate the client; a non-overlapped handle adopted this way hangs (as measured at chunk 2026-09-25-security-prerequisites). Rationale: one API over named pipes and Unix sockets, sync and Tokio flavours, and no daemon. macOS caps socket paths at about 104 bytes. Accepted risk: interprocess has one main maintainer, which the seam contains.
