ast 64 bytes of parameters or payload, and a C0 inside a sequence (which aborts it). A read ending on a lone ESC is the Esc key at once, and a sequence past `ESC [` / `ESC ]` / `ESC P` / `CSI M` carries across reads. The classifier only observes: the stdin observer (`run::wheel::Observed`, wrapping the host stdin handed to `viola_pty::pump_with_paste`) returns every byte whole and unchanged, and a read holding an editing key moves the wheel before it returns. On Windows the platform decides what arrives (the founder's live ruling F-W3, 2026-10-04, relayed by the overseer): the inbox ConPTY outer terminal swallows focus reports, and under the sideloaded ConPTY's win32-input-mode request (`ESC[?9001h`) a mouse report written as text into the console's input arrives as one win32 key-down record per character (`ESC[0;0;<Uc>;1;0;1_`) and takes the wheel — as measured on `windows-2025` (ci#37227518624) for injected bytes; a mouse report from a real Windows terminal is not yet measured. The error only ever favours the human. A bridge send is written as one `ESC[200~…ESC[201~` + CR write. Human bytes arriving during that write are held in the pump and passed on straight after. An unsent `prompt-submitted` confirms and logs the take: one the hook filed `origin:"human"` moves the wheel before its line is appended (a `harness` prompt and the relabelled in-flight `driver` prompt never move it). An in-flight send whose text ends in CR or LF characters is typed without them, and one whose text holds a CR or a CR LF inside is typed with each as one LF, so its own prompt matches, is relabelled `driver` and moves no wheel (as landed at chunk 2026-10-07-a-send-ending-in-a-newline-is-confirmed for LF, at chunk 2026-10-09-epoch-3-cleanup for CR and CRLF at the end, and at chunk 2026-10-09-inner-cr-and-crlf-in-a-sent-text for a CR or a CR LF inside; [Delivery Confirmation]). A prompt that is not the in-flight send's typed text claims nothing and is appended as the hook filed it, so one 